00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:15.680
Instead you need to look back
at the first year of Bitcoin,

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:18.270
back in January, 2008.

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:19.740
- Let's talk about the speed with which

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:21.430
we are watching this market deteriorate.

00:11:21.430 --> 00:11:24.740
- This could be the most
serious recession in decades.

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:27.090
- The economy was in a free fall.

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:29.870
Banks and regulators
had massively mismanaged

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:31.330
our financial system.

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:33.280
Governments were bailing out these banks

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:36.010
because they were "too big to fail".

00:11:36.010 --> 00:11:39.620
And out of nowhere, someone
writing under a pen name,

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:44.060
Satoshi Nakamoto, proposed
this idea of Bitcoin,

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:47.500
which was a peer-to-peer
electronic cash system,

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:50.490
something that wouldn't
need governments or banks.

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:53.280
The first block of code to start off this

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:56.600
decentralized record that
every Bitcoin transaction

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:57.940
would be built upon.

00:11:57.940 --> 00:12:00.930
In that first code, you
see a little Easter egg,

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:03.420
a headline from the day's news

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:05.570
of governments bailing out banks.

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:07.257
Nakamoto wrote that

00:12:07.257 --> 00:12:10.660
"the root problem with
conventional currency

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:14.320
is all the trust that is
required to make it work."

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:15.380
Bitcoin would solve this

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:19.453
using the same ethic that
had built the Internet.

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:23.470
Connecting strangers from around the world

00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:26.060
without the need of a middleman.

00:12:26.060 --> 00:12:29.660
So yes, this is useful
because detaching money

00:12:29.660 --> 00:12:32.517
from these big institutions
that have screwed it up

00:12:32.517 --> 00:12:34.970
is useful for a lot of things.

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:35.803
- Oh yeah, totally.

00:12:35.803 --> 00:12:37.500
You mean like buying drugs?

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:40.620
- Well, yes, but also
a bunch of other things

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:42.250
that you probably can't imagine

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:45.140
because you live here
in the United States.

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:47.670
Consider Nigeria for a second.

00:12:47.670 --> 00:12:51.250
Last year, the government
literally froze the bank accounts

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:54.070
of 20 people who were
protesting police brutality

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:54.903
in the country.

00:12:54.903 --> 00:12:57.730
But some of them were able
to still receive donations

00:12:57.730 --> 00:13:01.370
through Bitcoin because the
government couldn't freeze that.

00:13:01.370 --> 00:13:03.210
Look at another example, Venezuela,

00:13:03.210 --> 00:13:06.290
a place where a stack of
money is worth nothing

00:13:06.290 --> 00:13:07.680
because of hyperinflation.

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:10.320
- Purse is made entirely of the bills of

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:12.330
the Venezuelan currency, the bolívar.

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:14.410
Inflation is so high
that this money is now

00:13:14.410 --> 00:13:15.410
completely worthless.

00:13:15.410 --> 00:13:17.950
- This wiped out people's life savings,

00:13:17.950 --> 00:13:21.260
all because of trust in
these big institutions.

00:13:21.260 --> 00:13:23.630
These are problems that
don't happen with Bitcoin.

00:13:23.630 --> 00:13:26.210
There's no government that
can freeze your account.

00:13:26.210 --> 00:13:28.240
There's no government
that can print more money

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:30.550
and de-value your coins.

00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:34.200
It's not controlled by governments
or a team of executives.

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:36.720
It's controlled by the community.

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:39.960
The fact of the matter is,
all money is turning digital.

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:42.670
And let me guess: you probably
trust your central bank

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:45.010
and your government because
you live here in the US.

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:47.250
But according to the
Human Rights Foundation,

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:49.800
more than half the
world's population lives

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:51.720
under an authoritarian regime.

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:53.220
- I get it, I get it.

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:56.180
Removing banks and
governments as the middleman

00:13:56.180 --> 00:13:58.260
has some major upside,

00:13:58.260 --> 00:14:01.410
but who's going to protect
the Nigerians and Venezuelans

00:14:01.410 --> 00:14:04.760
from Bitcoin scammers and hackers?

00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:07.070
I mean, just look at this list.

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:10.130
This is a list of all of
the cryptocurrency exchanges

00:14:10.130 --> 00:14:11.570
that have been hacked.

00:14:11.570 --> 00:14:13.400
It's a really long list.

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:14.830
Imagine if you're just a normal consumer

00:14:14.830 --> 00:14:16.710
who isn't like a tech person,

00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:20.410
like if you don't have at least
some institutional buffer,

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:22.340
you're way more vulnerable.

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:24.260
But the blockchain it's so secure.

00:14:24.260 --> 00:14:25.440
It's public record.

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:27.620
It's verified through all
this fancy technology.

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:28.740
Yeah, the blockchain is.

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:31.040
The blockchain is a
fantastic, really cool thing,

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:34.690
but people aren't interacting
with the blockchain directly.

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:36.730
You still need all of these services

00:14:36.730 --> 00:14:38.660
to buy and sell your crypto.

00:14:38.660 --> 00:14:40.400
It's these third party companies

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:42.190
that turn into another middleman,

00:14:42.190 --> 00:14:45.740
vulnerable of being hacked,
and they are all the time.

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:48.590
But let's just say that your
crypto assets are totally safe.

00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:49.760
They never get hacked.

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:53.580
You're still subject to
insane levels of volatility.

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:55.440
I mean, look at Bitcoin's graph.

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:57.630
It's swinging all of the time.

00:14:57.630 --> 00:15:00.740
It swings by 30% or more,

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:03.500
which is great if you're
like a risk-tolerant guy

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:06.400
with a ton of money to
play with on the Internet,

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:08.173
this is a really fun thing to do.

00:15:08.327 --> 00:15:10.885
But what if you're somebody who
actually needs your currency

00:15:10.954 --> 00:15:13.824
to stay stable so that
you can know its value

00:15:14.420 --> 00:15:17.510
so that you can go buy,
I don't know, groceries?

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:20.780
So how do you control
these hyper volatile assets

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:22.810
so that they stay predictable?

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:26.880
Well, maybe some sort
of authority middlemen.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:28.960
And now we're right back
into the messy world

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:32.100
of trust and middlemen,
government control.

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:34.300
This is happening right
now in El Salvador.

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:36.980
- El Salvador became the
first country in the world

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:39.661
on Tuesday to make Bitcoin legal tender.

00:15:39.661 --> 00:15:42.020
(man speaking Spanish)

00:15:42.020 --> 00:15:42.853
- What does this mean for Bitcoin?

00:15:42.853 --> 00:15:45.210
What does this mean for El Salvador?

00:15:45.210 --> 00:15:47.870
- Earlier this year, the
president of El Salvador announced

00:15:47.870 --> 00:15:50.790
that Bitcoin would become
like an official currency,

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:53.070
like a legal form of money,

00:15:53.070 --> 00:15:54.579
and that every business in the country

00:15:54.579 --> 00:15:56.840
would have to accept it.

00:15:56.840 --> 00:15:58.000
- [Anchorman] It'll run as another option

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:00.580
alongside the current currency, US dollar.

00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:01.590
- But guess what?

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:04.270
All of the infrastructure
to actually use Bitcoin

00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:05.270
to buy things,

00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:07.701
the app where you can actually
exchange and buy Bitcoin,

00:16:07.701 --> 00:16:09.830
the ATMs to withdraw the cash,

00:16:09.830 --> 00:16:12.830
it's controlled by the
national government.

00:16:12.830 --> 00:16:15.160
It's like crypto is fantastic in theory,

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:17.570
and for a select few people
who have disposable income

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:18.860
to play around with,

00:16:18.860 --> 00:16:20.800
but it rarely happens in practice

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:22.950
without the same old
dynamics of a middleman

00:16:22.950 --> 00:16:24.780
coming in to make sure
it works for the masses.

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:29.670
- Oh, gosh, you're focusing
so much on how it works NOW.

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:31.440
I mean, if you were to apply that logic,

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:34.463
like go back to the Internet 1.0.

00:16:34.463 --> 00:16:35.430
- [Woman] Information super-highway.

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:38.720
- [Man] Everything unique for
great Internet experience.

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:41.000
- Wake up and smell the '90s.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:43.950
- You have an Internet
browser in the 1990s.

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:46.090
We have the birth of a website.

00:16:46.090 --> 00:16:48.435
It is a thing that you
log onto the Internet.

00:16:48.435 --> 00:16:51.990
You read things, you
click links and that's it.

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:54.290
It is a read-only experience.

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:55.980
People could have thought
like, oh, that's the Internet.

00:16:55.980 --> 00:16:57.190
Like, this is what we do.

00:16:57.190 --> 00:16:58.440
We go read things.

00:16:58.440 --> 00:16:59.623
- This is boring.

00:16:59.623 --> 00:17:01.190
- It's just a digital encyclopedia

00:17:01.190 --> 00:17:03.120
that's connected to
other people's computers.

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:04.680
- I don't need a history lesson.

00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:06.130
Where are you going with this?

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:07.870
- Just hear me out for a second.

00:17:07.870 --> 00:17:08.730
- Yeah, hear him out.

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:09.563
Come on.

00:17:09.563 --> 00:17:10.527
- Wait a minute.

00:17:10.527 --> 00:17:11.360
You're still here?

00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:12.193
- Yeah, I'm still here.

00:17:12.193 --> 00:17:13.026
I've been here the whole time,

00:17:13.026 --> 00:17:15.340
trying to make up my
mind as you two argue.

00:17:15.340 --> 00:17:16.173
Go ahead.

00:17:16.173 --> 00:17:17.006
- Okay.

00:17:17.006 --> 00:17:18.770
So that was the Internet in the '90s,

00:17:18.770 --> 00:17:20.637
but then we got Web Two.

00:17:20.637 --> 00:17:23.970
(electronic music playing)

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:26.470
In the 2000s, you start to see webpages go

00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:28.760
from static to dynamic.

00:17:28.760 --> 00:17:31.112
Users can now generate their own content

00:17:31.112 --> 00:17:33.950
from blogs to uploading to My Space,

00:17:33.950 --> 00:17:36.180
and then Facebook and YouTube.

00:17:36.180 --> 00:17:38.330
They're now participating in the Internet.

00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:40.110
They're up-voting comments.

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:41.120
That's where we are now.

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:42.370
We're in a part of the Internet

00:17:42.370 --> 00:17:44.270
where you're actually participating.

00:17:44.270 --> 00:17:46.620
We are creating the Internet together,

00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:50.370
but in doing so, we
created these centralized

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:54.780
digital kingdoms owned
by giant tech companies

00:17:54.780 --> 00:17:57.380
who, in exchange for us
being able to participate

00:17:57.380 --> 00:17:58.880
in this cool new Internet,

00:17:58.880 --> 00:18:00.020
they take our data.

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:02.580
They show us ads and they
make billions and billions

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:03.712
and billions of dollars.

00:18:03.712 --> 00:18:05.720
(joyous music)

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:07.070
Sometimes they share a little bit of

00:18:07.070 --> 00:18:09.730
those billions of dollars
with the creators,

00:18:09.730 --> 00:18:11.810
but most of us are doing free work

00:18:11.810 --> 00:18:14.650
for these big tech companies
so that we can participate

00:18:14.650 --> 00:18:17.940
and make content for their
interactive web pages.

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:20.363
There is a better way, Web Three.

00:18:21.650 --> 00:18:23.280
This is what we're moving into.

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:25.910
It'll allow us to move
money, make purchases,

00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:28.300
connect with people
without the permission of

00:18:28.300 --> 00:18:30.310
Mark Zuckerberg or JP Morgan

00:18:30.310 --> 00:18:32.460
or the government of the United States.

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:34.220
They're not a part of
this equation at all.

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:36.830
It is a vision for an
entirely new type of Internet.

00:18:36.830 --> 00:18:37.980
- Do you hear yourself?

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:41.210
You sound like some
authoritarian politician

00:18:41.210 --> 00:18:44.166
peddling some utopian
vision for the future.

00:18:44.166 --> 00:18:45.880
Let's get back to facts.

00:18:45.880 --> 00:18:46.713
- Wait, no.

00:18:46.713 --> 00:18:47.890
This is actually happening.

00:18:47.890 --> 00:18:50.250
This isn't some nebulous
vision for the future.

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:51.960
We get it, Bitcoin was a currency

00:18:51.960 --> 00:18:53.410
that is inherently limited,

00:18:53.410 --> 00:18:55.480
but Ethereum is a new technology

00:18:55.480 --> 00:18:58.620
that uses the same concept
of getting rid of middlemen.

00:18:58.620 --> 00:19:01.620
But instead of just allowing
us to do the currency thing,

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:03.900
it allows you to do tons of things

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:06.270
that we used to need institutions for.

00:19:06.270 --> 00:19:08.180
It's something called a token.

00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:10.600
- Oh gosh, here we go again with tokens.

00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:12.260
Tokens, tokens, tokens.

00:19:12.260 --> 00:19:14.570
Tokens, let me guess, they're
going to save the world.

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:15.960
They're going to change everything.

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:18.830
Everything is solved by
tokens on the blockchain.

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:22.650
All I see tokens being used
for is selling digital art

00:19:22.650 --> 00:19:24.820
for millions of dollars to speculators

00:19:24.820 --> 00:19:26.430
who want attention on the Internet.

00:19:26.430 --> 00:19:29.670
- Yes, making digital things
scarce on the Internet

00:19:29.670 --> 00:19:32.820
is one of the first major
applications for this.

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:34.470
But this is just the beginning.

00:19:34.470 --> 00:19:35.790
You got to think ahead, man.

00:19:35.790 --> 00:19:36.720
Think about it.

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:40.997
Borrowing money, signing
contracts, investing.

00:19:40.997 --> 00:19:43.680
There's so many things that we do

00:19:43.680 --> 00:19:46.080
where there are huge
middlemen in the center

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:48.780
who were making tons of
money off of ensuring

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:50.780
that the transaction goes properly.

00:19:50.780 --> 00:19:51.692
- I'll take that back.

00:19:51.692 --> 00:19:52.525
(woman screaming)

00:19:52.525 --> 00:19:53.710
I mean, just imagine an Internet

00:19:53.710 --> 00:19:56.980
that is owned and governed by the users.

00:19:56.980 --> 00:19:59.280
Instead of logging onto
each of these platforms

00:19:59.280 --> 00:20:01.050
with your separate username,

00:20:01.050 --> 00:20:03.030
giving over all of your data,

00:20:03.030 --> 00:20:05.460
you log onto one unique wallet

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:07.471
that holds all of your
personal information,

00:20:07.471 --> 00:20:10.790
all of your crypto
assets that are protected

00:20:10.790 --> 00:20:13.240
by this very sophisticated cryptography.

00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:15.380
Your data isn't owned and stored

00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:17.350
by these giant tech companies.

00:20:17.350 --> 00:20:20.460
But instead, it is stored
on encrypted ledgers

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:22.790
and you get to decide
who you share it with

00:20:22.790 --> 00:20:24.410
and how to value it.

00:20:24.410 --> 00:20:26.600
I'm telling you, I know it's really early.

00:20:26.600 --> 00:20:29.988
And all you're seeing is like
millions of dollar cat gifs.

00:20:29.988 --> 00:20:31.460
(joyous music)

00:20:31.460 --> 00:20:34.490
But what this is doing is
fueling a creative boom

00:20:34.490 --> 00:20:36.870
among really bright developers,

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:38.390
which in 10 years from now

00:20:38.390 --> 00:20:41.280
will have totally
transformed the Internet.

00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:45.080
- I hear you painting this
picture of some Internet utopia,

00:20:45.080 --> 00:20:46.820
where everyone has a voice.

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:49.210
I would love a world where users actually

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:52.100
have ownership over what happens online

00:20:52.100 --> 00:20:54.833
and they can share
decision-making and revenue.

00:20:56.270 --> 00:20:58.160
And while we're talking about blockchain,

00:20:58.160 --> 00:21:00.630
this cure-all technology that
people just throw around,

00:21:00.630 --> 00:21:02.020
like it's going to save everything.

00:21:02.020 --> 00:21:04.340
Let us remember that in reality,

00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:07.920
blockchain is secured with cryptography,

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:11.310
which is actually very
inefficient by design.

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:14.010
If you really want decentralization,

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:17.770
you need to have a network
that is redundant and slow

00:21:17.770 --> 00:21:20.380
and hard to navigate without a third party

00:21:20.380 --> 00:21:21.660
being your middleman.

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:24.640
You, the user have to
trust in these companies

00:21:24.640 --> 00:21:26.570
to run things smoothly.

00:21:26.570 --> 00:21:28.540
So why do you need a blockchain anymore,

00:21:28.540 --> 00:21:31.930
when now you're trusting
a third-party company

00:21:31.930 --> 00:21:34.240
to do all this work for you?

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:36.950
I think it's a much more
narrow tool than you think.

00:21:36.950 --> 00:21:38.870
And a lot of people are
going to lose a lot of money

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:41.430
before they realize that that's the case.

00:21:41.430 --> 00:21:43.190
- Woo, okay.

00:21:43.190 --> 00:21:44.260
Let's all take a deep breath.

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:46.730
That was a lot.

00:21:46.730 --> 00:21:49.230
And as impassioned as this got,

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:51.982
I promise you it's a
hundred times more intense

00:21:51.982 --> 00:21:54.530
in Twitter feeds around the Internet.

00:21:54.530 --> 00:21:57.910
People feel very strongly
on both sides of this.

00:21:57.910 --> 00:21:59.300
You'll probably be
seeing it in the comments

00:21:59.300 --> 00:22:00.133
here pretty soon.

00:22:00.133 --> 00:22:03.210
I want to know which of
these people you agree with.

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:06.400
Do you agree with the
optimistic view of crypto,

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:08.790
or do you think that this is just another

00:22:08.790 --> 00:22:11.410
obscure technology that will have its day

00:22:11.410 --> 00:22:13.540
and be a bubble that eventually bursts?

00:22:13.540 --> 00:22:15.330
For me, I'm still learning.

00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:18.240
I'm going to drink in as much
information on this topic

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:21.890
as it develops because we're
still in the early stages.

00:22:21.890 --> 00:22:23.370
And I actually don't think it's healthy

00:22:23.370 --> 00:22:26.470
that we have to make
our minds up on topics

00:22:26.470 --> 00:22:29.190
that are still really, really theoretical.

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:32.780
To me, it's a much better idea to sit back

00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:35.410
and take in the information as it comes.

00:22:35.410 --> 00:22:38.820
And eventually you'll start
to develop your own opinion.

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:41.450
You should be compelled
to do so by the evidence,

00:22:41.450 --> 00:22:43.830
by the data that speaks to you,

00:22:43.830 --> 00:22:47.300
not by angry bullies in Twitter feeds.

00:22:47.300 --> 00:22:49.073
So, keep your mind open,

00:22:49.925 --> 00:22:52.110
but not so open that your brain falls out.

00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:53.160
Be careful out there.

00:22:54.023 --> 00:22:56.690
(gentle music)

00:23:05.240 --> 00:23:10.240
- Don't forget to, uh, buy
Dogecoin and buy, um, Ethereum. Ugh!

